  "draftIsFinal": false,
  "summary": "I wrote `src/main.ts`, and `typecheck` passes. I couldn't run `vite build` or open the page, so I haven't seen it render. The GLSL has never been compiled, so a shader error would only show up in the browser.\n\n- **Water:** a custom `material.custom` GLSL shader on a subdivided grid of about 57k vertices, with rows packed densely near the camera and spreading out toward the horizon.\n  - Vertex displacement: five summed sine waves move the geometry, with the small waves fading with distance.\n  - Procedural ripple: noise-based detail perturbs the normal on top of the wave normal.\n  - Fresnel: a Schlick term mixes a dark blue-green water body with the reflected sky.\n  - Sun specular glint: a tight lobe plus a broad sheen.\n  - Horizon haze: far water fades into the horizon colour so it meets the sky without a seam.\n- **Sky:** a gradient dome with procedural clouds and a sun disc. The water calls the same `skyColor()` function, so what it reflects is exactly what the dome shows.\n- **Animation:** the wave time is a `waveClock` uniform that I set each frame from `performance.now()` through `app.nodes.require(\"water\").setUniform`. I didn't rely on the engine's built-in `u_time`, because I couldn't confirm it advances in headless Chromium.\n- **Camera:** it sits 2 units above the water and looks slightly down, so the horizon lands about 38% from the top.\n- **Colour output:** the shaders write linear light and the app uses `output: { toneMapping: \"neutral\", exposure: 1 }`. This is meant to keep the hues from going grey and to roll off the bright sun glint.\n- **Contract:** `resize: false` and `pixelRatio: 1` keep the 640x360 canvas as it is. `window.__EVAL_READY__` is set two animation frames after the first frame callback, as a stand-in for \"first frame drawn\".\n\n",
